<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/003V">Definition <span data-tag="003V">1.4.3.1</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $f,g: C \rightarrow D$ be a pair of morphisms in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ having the same source and target. A <em>homotopy from $f$ to $g$</em> is a $2$-simplex $\sigma $ of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ satisfying $d^{2}_0( \sigma ) = \operatorname{id}_{D}$, $d^{2}_1(\sigma ) = g$, and $d^{2}_2(\sigma ) = f$, as depicted in the diagram </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ &amp;  D \ar [dr]^{\operatorname{id}_ D} &amp;  \\ C \ar [ur]^{f} \ar [rr]^{g} &amp;  &amp;  D. } \]
</div>
<p> We will say that $f$ and $g$ are <em>homotopic</em> if there exists a homotopy from $f$ to $g$. </p>
</article>
